  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Saturday)\nIssued at 1236 PM MDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nUpper level analysis shows broad southwesterly flow across the \nNorthern Rockies into the Northern Plains. Deep troughing is noted\nacross the western US with closed upper low dropping into WA with\na second closed low off the southern CA coast. Surface low \npressure is centered over central ND with a cold front extending \nwestward through southeastern MT, slowly pushing into far \nnorthwestern SD. Surface winds in northwestern SD have shifted out\nof the northwest, with west-southwesterly winds across the rest \nof the CWA. Very hot today with widespread 80s and to low 90s, \nwarmest across the western SD plains.\n\nIsolated showers and a few lightning strikes are possible later this \nafternoon and evening hours as shortwave trough pushes into the \nregion. Given dry boundary layer, lack of appreciable moisture, and \nisolated coverage, precipitation amounts will be light and spotty.\nHowever, gusty winds are possible with any precipitation cores. \nThe cold front will settle southward into the forecast area \ntonight. Cooler temperatures behind the front, although frontal \nposition a bit uncertain Sunday. Current thinking is that the \nfront will be slow to push south of the area, allowing for warmer \ntemperatures along SD/NE border (70s) and cooler temperatures \nnorthward through Sunday afternoon.\n\nCold front will shift south of the area by Monday with cooler, yet \nnear seasonable temperatures through the rest of the week. Active \nsouthwesterly flow aloft coupled with moisture influx from the \nremnants of Tropical Storm Rachel will support precipitation chances \nlate Sunday into Monday, although best precipitation chances will be \ndisplaced north and northwest of the forecast area. A deep upper low \nis forecast to push through the area late Tuesday into Wednesday \nwith another chance of precipitation Wednesday.\n\nForecast confidence wanes beyond Wednesday with considerable spread \namong the models/ensembles regarding the synoptic evolution.",
